\description{
How the communities of treated samples diverge from those of a control
over time, summarized as one curve per treatment (van den Brink & ter
Braak 1999). The method is a partial redundancy analysis,
\code{rda(counts ~ treatment:time + Condition(time))}: the effect of time is
removed, and what remains is the treatments' effect at each time point.
Each treatment's curve is its canonical coefficient at each time point,
measured from the control (whose curve is 0), and each feature's weight
says how closely its abundance follows the curves. Gives the same results
as \code{vegan::prc()}, with permutation tests of the first axis and of all
axes as \code{anova(vegan::prc(...), first = TRUE)} and \code{anova(...)}.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{Z} the time points' indicators (the conditions) and \eqn{X} the
indicators of each treatment other than the control at each time point
(the constraints), PRC is the partial RDA of the normalized table on
\eqn{X} given \eqn{Z} (van den Brink & ter Braak 1999): \code{rda(counts, data = d, formula = ~ treatment:time + Condition(time))}, which spans the
same model. Its fitted values are each cell's (treatment \eqn{\times}
time point's) mean, less the mean of its time point, so the site scores
are constant within a cell. A treatment's canonical coefficient at a time
point is its cell's site score less the control's at that time point
(vegan's \eqn{c_{dt}}), here averaged over the cell's samples (the "wa"
scores of \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}}, whose cell means are the fitted scores). The features'
weights \eqn{b_k} are the species scores, so that
\eqn{b_k c_{dt}} approximates the change in feature \eqn{k}'s
(normalized) abundance under treatment \eqn{d} at time \eqn{t}, relative
to the control.

Use \code{norm} to analyze transformed abundances, such as \code{norm = 'clr'} (with
a \code{pseudocount}) or \code{norm = 'hellinger'}. van den Brink & ter Braak
analyzed \eqn{\log(10 x + 1)} counts; transform such values yourself and
give them with \code{norm = 'none'}, the default.

\strong{Permutation tests}

Both tests are those of \code{vegan::anova.cca()} under the reduced model:
\itemize{
\item all axes: \eqn{F = (\mathrm{SS}_X / q) / (\mathrm{RSS} / \nu)},
for \eqn{q} constraint columns and \eqn{\nu = n - 1 - \mathrm{rank}(Z) -
  q} residual degrees of freedom. This is PERMANOVA of the Euclidean
distances between the normalized samples (\code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}} of
\code{\link[=euclidean]{euclidean()}}), with the indicators of the treatments other than the
control at each time point as constraints and time as the condition:
the columns of \code{model.matrix(~ time + time:treatment)} after time's.
(\code{formula = ~ treatment:time + Condition(time)} spans the same model,
so it has the same F, but it keeps other columns of the aliased
model matrix, whose permutations give other permuted F values.)
\item the first axis (\code{first = TRUE} in \code{vegan}): \eqn{F = \lambda_1 /
  (\mathrm{RSS} / \nu)}, with \eqn{\lambda_1} the first constrained
eigenvalue and RSS the residual of the full model.
}

Each permutation shuffles the rows of the constraints (equivalently, the
samples), removes the conditions from them, and refits the model, so that
the p-values equal \code{vegan}'s for the same permutations. Samples sampled
repeatedly are not independent, so permute whole plots (\code{plots}) or
within time points (\code{strata}) rather than freely; \code{vegan}'s own
restricted designs (such as \code{Within(type = "series")}) can be given as a
\code{permutations} matrix from \code{permute::shuffleSet()}.

\strong{Signs}

Each axis is flipped so that its coefficient with the largest absolute
value is positive (the first such, if tied), and the features' weights
and the \code{rda} element's scores, loadings, biplot scores and centroids are
flipped with it. So the curves mostly rise from the control, and their
signs may be the reverse of \code{vegan}'s.

\strong{Matching vegan}

\code{coefficients} and \code{weights} are \code{vegan}'s \code{summary(prc(...), scaling = scaling)} (up to each axis's sign), and \code{eig} its eigenvalues. Where a
time point has no control samples, \code{vegan::prc()} drops some other
treatment's column and reports coefficients relative to that treatment;
here they are \code{NA}, with a warning. The test statistics and p-values are
those of \code{anova(prc, first = TRUE)} and \code{anova(prc)} for the same
permutations.
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # Four treatments and a control, each in two plots, at five times.
    set.seed(1)
    meta <- expand.grid(plot = 1:10, time = c(-1, 1, 2, 4, 8))
    meta$dose <- rep(c(0, 0.1, 1, 10, 100), 2)[meta$plot]
    effect <- outer(log1p(meta$dose) * (meta$time > 0) / (1 + meta$time / 4), 1:12)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(50 * 12, exp(2 + 0.05 * outer(meta$time, 1:12 - 6) - 0.05 * effect)), 50)
    colnames(counts) <- paste0('Sp', 1:12)
    rownames(counts) <- rownames(meta) <- paste0('S', 1:50)

    prc <- principal_response_curves(
      log1p(counts), 'dose', 'time', meta, plots = 'plot', permutations = 99 )
    prc
    plot(prc)

    # The coefficients, ready for ggplot2.
    head(prc$coefficients)

}
